WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.452
Here's a simple truth that's easy to forget: learning takes time.

2
00:00:05.452 --> 00:00:10.377
Today we look at how much time California students get, and how well it's used.

3
00:00:10.377 --> 00:00:11.612
Let's start early.

4
00:00:11.612 --> 00:00:22.037
The years before age five are when the brain develops fastest, and children arrive at kindergarten with huge differences in vocabulary and early skills.

5
00:00:22.037 --> 00:00:24.987
Good early education can narrow those gaps.

6
00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:26.427
The key word is good.

7
00:00:26.427 --> 00:00:33.355
Studies of high-quality programs show lasting benefits, while mediocre programs show gains that fade.

8
00:00:33.355 --> 00:00:35.756
California has made a big bet here.

9
00:00:35.756 --> 00:00:45.564
The state has been phasing in transitional kindergarten, often called TK, a free year of school for four-year-olds before regular kindergarten.

10
00:00:45.564 --> 00:00:52.560
As of the 2025–26 school year, TK became available to all four-year-olds in California public schools.

11
00:00:52.560 --> 00:01:02.986
The challenge now is quality: enough trained teachers, small enough classes, and classrooms designed for young children, not just shrunken first grades.

12
00:01:02.986 --> 00:01:04.139
Next, class size.

13
00:01:04.139 --> 00:01:13.633
Smaller classes sound obviously better, and in the early grades there's good evidence that they help, especially for disadvantaged students.

14
00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:20.822
But class size reduction is expensive, because every smaller class needs another teacher and another room.

15
00:01:20.822 --> 00:01:25.570
In the late 1990s, California funded a big push to shrink K–3 classes.

16
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:34.115
It needed so many new teachers so fast that many under-prepared teachers were hired, and the benefits were smaller than hoped.

17
00:01:34.115 --> 00:01:43.677
The lesson: class size matters, but it's not the only way to give students more individual attention, and it can crowd out other investments.

18
00:01:43.677 --> 00:01:45.541
How long is the school day?

19
00:01:45.541 --> 00:01:53.341
California sets minimum instructional minutes by grade, and the state's school year is typically around 180 days.

20
00:01:53.341 --> 00:01:57.758
It turns out that's not short by international standards at all.

21
00:01:57.758 --> 00:02:07.836
Compared with most other countries, American students actually spend more time in the classroom, not less, according to data compiled by the OECD.

22
00:02:07.836 --> 00:02:11.770
So the real challenge isn't a shortage of scheduled time.

23
00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.567
It's what happens to that time once the doors are open.

24
00:02:15.567 --> 00:02:22.814
A lot of it disappears into transitions, announcements, assemblies, test prep, and classroom disruptions.

25
00:02:22.814 --> 00:02:32.823
Researchers who've followed a class through a day often find the real amount of focused learning time is much smaller than the schedule suggests.

26
00:02:32.823 --> 00:02:38.000
Protecting that time is one of the cheapest improvements a school can make.

27
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:59.812
California has also used the schedule itself as a tool for helping students: a 2019 law, SB 328, requires California high schools to start no earlier than 8:30 in the morning and middle schools no earlier than 8:00, reflecting research that teenagers in particular do better in school when they get a bit more sleep.

28
00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:06.108
When students fall behind, the most effective single fix in the research is intensive tutoring.

29
00:03:06.108 --> 00:03:17.772
Not homework help once a week, but frequent sessions, several times a week, in small groups of one to four, during the school day, with a consistent tutor using good materials.

30
00:03:17.772 --> 00:03:22.411
Some programs have shown gains equivalent to months of extra learning.

31
00:03:22.411 --> 00:03:30.827
After the pandemic, many districts invested in tutoring, with mixed results depending on how closely they followed that recipe.

32
00:03:30.827 --> 00:03:35.731
Summer is the other big lever, though it comes with a caveat worth naming.

33
00:03:35.731 --> 00:03:47.660
The idea of the "summer slide," where students lose ground academically over a long break, gets repeated often, but researchers actually disagree about how real or how large it is.

34
00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.504
Some studies do find a genuine loss, particularly in math.

35
00:03:51.504 --> 00:04:00.119
Others argue that much of what looks like decline is really just a temporary dip that fades once the school year gets going again.

36
00:04:00.119 --> 00:04:11.319
What's less disputed is that kids without access to camps, books, and enrichment simply have fewer chances to keep learning and growing over the summer than kids who do.

37
00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:19.139
Summer programs can help close that gap, but only if students actually attend and the program has real learning in it.

38
00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:23.447
After-school programs fill a similar role during the school year.

39
00:04:23.447 --> 00:04:39.286
California funds a large network of them through a program called ELO-P, the Expanded Learning Opportunities Program, which aims to give students in many districts access to a full nine-hour day of school and enrichment, plus summer weeks.

40
00:04:39.286 --> 00:04:43.925
Good programs mix homework help, arts, sports, and a safe place to be.

41
00:04:43.925 --> 00:04:47.537
Finally, the most basic time issue of all: showing up.

42
00:04:47.537 --> 00:04:55.430
A student who misses ten percent or more of school days, about eighteen days a year, is considered chronically absent.

43
00:04:55.430 --> 00:05:02.186
Chronic absence rose sharply after the pandemic and is still well above earlier levels in California.

44
00:05:02.186 --> 00:05:09.946
Missing school early is linked to weaker reading by third grade, and missing school later is linked to dropping out.

45
00:05:09.946 --> 00:05:28.876
There's also a direct financial reason this matters for schools, not just for students: California funds districts based largely on average daily attendance, not simply how many students are enrolled, so when kids aren't in their seats, it's the school's budget that takes a hit too.

46
00:05:28.876 --> 00:05:38.107
The causes of absence are often solvable: illness, transportation, family crises, or a student who feels no one would notice they're gone.

47
00:05:38.107 --> 00:05:47.606
Schools that track attendance closely and reach out personally, especially with a friendly call rather than a threat, tend to bring kids back.

48
00:05:47.606 --> 00:05:51.753
For more, see Chapter 4 of the free Ed100 course at ed100.org.
